Java modules — the sixty-second tour.

For most of Java's life, the largest unit of code was the package, and public meant visible to everyone, everywhere. That's leaky. A library's internal helper classes had to be public just to be shared across its own packages — which also exposed them to every consumer. Java nine introduced the module, the java platform module system, to draw a real boundary around a set of packages. This is the sixty-second tour: enough to recognize a module, and know what problem it solves.

A module is a named group of packages, described by one file at its root, called module-info-dot-java. It declares three things: the module's name, what it requires — its dependencies — and what it exports, the packages it lets others see. So a module might declare its name, then require a payments module it depends on, then export its own public A-P-I package. And here's the crucial part — it's the line that isn't there. A package you don't export is inaccessible from outside the module, even its public classes. So public now means public within what the module chooses to expose. That's the strong encapsulation packages never had.

So modules buy you two things. First, strong encapsulation: internal packages are genuinely hidden. A library can refactor or delete un-exported classes freely, knowing no outside code could have depended on them. Public stops meaning global. Second, reliable configuration: because every module states its dependencies with requires, the virtual machine verifies the whole dependency graph is present and consistent at startup — not when you happen to hit a missing class. This replaces the old classpath hell — the no-class-def-found error you'd only discover at runtime.

The most important user of modules is the java development kit itself. Since Java nine, the platform is split into modules — java-dot-base, which is always present and holds String, the collections, and Object; then java-dot-sequel, java-dot-x-m-l, and so on. That modularization is what enables j-link — a tool that assembles a custom runtime image containing only the modules your app actually uses. That's a smaller, faster-starting virtual machine, which matters a lot for containers and cloud deployment.

Now be honest about scope. Most applications still run on the classpath, not the module path, and get along fine. Modules earn their keep in two places: the platform itself, and large, long-lived systems or libraries that need enforced boundaries and slim custom runtimes. So the realistic goal for now is recognition. When you open a project and see a module-info-dot-java, you know it declares a module's name, its required dependencies, and its exported packages — and that un-exported packages are sealed off. That's the sixty-second tour. Modules are about compile-time and startup-time boundaries. The next two sections go the opposite direction — code that inspects and manipulates other code at runtime: reflection, and the annotations that ride on it.
